1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,695
Amplitude is the loudness of a radio wave.

2
00:00:04,695 --> 00:00:09,955
Formally, it's the magnitude — the height — of the wave: the peak departure of its

3
00:00:09,955 --> 00:00:12,201
oscillation from the resting level.

4
00:00:12,201 --> 00:00:17,205
For radio it corresponds to signal strength, the thing a receiver reports as a

5
00:00:17,205 --> 00:00:17,975
power level.

6
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:23,107
And it's one of exactly three properties — with frequency and phase — that fully

7
00:00:23,107 --> 00:00:27,598
describe a wave, and that a transmitter can vary to carry information.

8
00:00:28,048 --> 00:00:30,781
The everyday anchor: a whisper and a shout.

9
00:00:30,781 --> 00:00:35,866
Same words, same pitch, same pace — the only difference is how hard the air gets

10
00:00:35,866 --> 00:00:36,311
pushed.

11
00:00:36,311 --> 00:00:37,391
That's amplitude.

12
00:00:37,391 --> 00:00:42,476
Nothing about the message changes when a signal is stronger; what changes is how

13
00:00:42,476 --> 00:00:47,180
much energy arrives to carry it above everything else that's shouting too.

14
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:52,273
Now the one relationship worth engraving: amplitude and power are linked by a

15
00:00:52,273 --> 00:00:52,695
square.

16
00:00:52,695 --> 00:00:57,277
The power a wave carries is proportional to the square of its amplitude — so

17
00:00:57,277 --> 00:00:59,931
doubling the amplitude quadruples the power.

18
00:00:59,931 --> 00:01:04,574
That square is why engineers stop talking about amplitude the moment a signal

19
00:01:04,574 --> 00:01:08,975
reaches a receiver and switch to power instead, and why levels are quoted

20
00:01:08,975 --> 00:01:13,799
logarithmically in decibels: a scale that turns those wide multiplicative ranges

21
00:01:13,799 --> 00:01:15,367
into manageable additions.

22
00:01:15,817 --> 00:01:21,251
A refinement: a single clean sinusoid can be summarised by its peak amplitude, but

23
00:01:21,251 --> 00:01:26,553
real signals are messy, so engineers more often use the RMS — root-mean-square —

24
00:01:26,553 --> 00:01:29,932
amplitude, which relates directly to average power.

25
00:01:29,932 --> 00:01:34,969
The gap between peak and average, the crest factor, is what decides how much

26
00:01:34,969 --> 00:01:36,825
headroom an amplifier needs.

27
00:01:37,275 --> 00:01:39,918
And amplitude was radio's first mailbox.

28
00:01:39,918 --> 00:01:44,875
The slowly varying outline traced by a modulated carrier's amplitude is its

29
00:01:44,875 --> 00:01:50,097
envelope, and amplitude modulation — AM — writes the message directly into that

30
00:01:50,097 --> 00:01:54,194
envelope, so simply that a lone diode detector can recover it.

31
00:01:54,194 --> 00:01:57,499
Varying amplitude is still one axis of modern QAM.

32
00:01:57,949 --> 00:02:01,064
Amplitude is also the property the journey eats.

33
00:02:01,064 --> 00:02:06,061
As a wave spreads out, its amplitude falls — in free space with the square of

34
00:02:06,061 --> 00:02:10,863
distance — and obstacles, absorption, and destructive multipath take more.

35
00:02:10,863 --> 00:02:16,249
That steady weakening is why a distant station is faint and why link budgets exist.

36
00:02:16,249 --> 00:02:21,505
At the receiver, what's left competes with the ever-present noise floor, and that

37
00:02:21,505 --> 00:02:26,761
ratio — signal to noise — ultimately bounds whether the message can be recovered.

38
00:02:27,211 --> 00:02:29,736
Why you care day to day: gain staging.

39
00:02:29,736 --> 00:02:35,052
Your front-end amplifiers and attenuators set where the signal's amplitude lands

40
00:02:35,052 --> 00:02:37,311
relative to the converter's range.

41
00:02:37,311 --> 00:02:40,567
Too little, and it sinks into quantisation noise.

42
00:02:40,567 --> 00:02:45,950
Too much, and it clips — generating intermodulation products that trash the whole

43
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:46,282
band.

44
00:02:46,282 --> 00:02:51,797
Automatic gain control exists to hold it in the sweet spot, because amplitude isn't

45
00:02:51,797 --> 00:02:57,313
static: multipath and motion make it fade and flutter, and receivers must ride that

46
00:02:57,313 --> 00:02:57,578
out.

47
00:02:58,028 --> 00:03:02,952
Inside GopherTrunk, the wave is IQ samples, and a sample's amplitude is its

48
00:03:02,952 --> 00:03:08,204
distance from the origin of the IQ plane — the magnitude, root of I-squared plus

49
00:03:08,204 --> 00:03:08,860
Q-squared.

50
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:13,981
The signal meter in dBFS is that number, reported against the converter's full

51
00:03:13,981 --> 00:03:14,375
scale.

52
00:03:14,375 --> 00:03:19,758
GopherTrunk reads it to judge whether a channel is active, to drive automatic gain

53
00:03:19,758 --> 00:03:24,025
control, and to compute the SNR estimates that gate weak decodes.

54
00:03:24,025 --> 00:03:29,342
The four-level and π/4-DQPSK signals it decodes are largely constant-envelope, so

55
00:03:29,342 --> 00:03:34,397
there amplitude serves as a health and gain-staging indicator rather than the

56
00:03:34,397 --> 00:03:35,644
information itself.

57
00:03:36,094 --> 00:03:41,410
So: amplitude is the height of the wave, power grows with its square, and in an SDR

58
00:03:41,410 --> 00:03:46,342
it becomes the magnitude of every IQ sample — the number your signal meter is

59
00:03:46,342 --> 00:03:47,111
showing you.

60
00:03:47,111 --> 00:03:48,840
Full write-up linked below.

